{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>The drink-offering of strong drink.</b> Wine that has fermented for forty days, excluding wine from the press (יין מגתו); thus explained Rashi, of blessed memory." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote concerning this that this is not correct, for they already said: 'wine from the press (יין מגתו) one may not bring, but if he did bring it, it is valid.' So it is not excluded from the verse at all, but is a rabbinic enhancement for the preferred manner לכתחלה; rather, 'strong drink' (שכר) serves to exclude diluted wine (מזוג), which is disqualified even after the fact (דיעבד). And so it is expounded in the Sifrei. And there they said: 'in the Holy — you shall pour a drink-offering in the Holy, and in the Holy they shall be absorbed,' end of quotation. And the language is doubled, 'הסך נסך' (you shall pour a drink-offering), to allude to two drink-offerings: the pouring of the water and the pouring of the wine." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'את קרבני לחמי' — this section was juxtaposed to the section of 'יפקוד ה׳.' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: 'While you command Me concerning My children, command My children concerning Me' — this is what the verse says (Isaiah 45:11): ״האותיות שאלוני על בני ועל פעל ידי תצוני״. A parable: to a king who married a wife and had a matchmaker (שושבין). All the time that the king was angry at his wife, the matchmaker would appease the king and reconcile him to the wife. When his time came to die, he began to request of the king, saying: 'I beseech you, give your attention to your wife.' The king said to him: 'Why do you command me concerning my wife? Command my wife concerning me — that she be careful with my honor.'" "<b>My offering, my food.</b> Two lambs every day, and not both of them at once, but 'the one lamb you shall do in the morning and the second lamb you shall do between the evenings.' And Solomon's daily bread was far more than this, as it says (I Kings 5:2–3): ״ויהי לחם שלמה ליום אחד שלשים כור סלת וששים כור קמח עשרה בקר מריאים״. This is what the verse says (Proverbs 13:25): ״צדיק אוכל לשובע נפשו ובטן רשעים תחסר״ — 'the righteous eats to satisfy his soul,' this is Eliezer, servant of Abraham, who said to Rebekah (Genesis 24:17): ״הגמיאיני נא מעט מים״; 'and the belly of the wicked lacks,' this is Esau, who said to Jacob (ibid. 25): ״הלעיטני נא״. One verse says (Job 37:23): ״שדי לא מצאנוהו שגיא כח״, and another verse says (ibid. 36): ״הן אל ישגיב בכחו ומי כמוהו מורה״. How can both verses stand? Rather, when He gives to Israel, He gives them according to His power, and when He asks of them, He asks only according to their power. See what is written (Exodus 26:1): ״ואת המשכן תעשה עשר יריעות״ — therefore it is written ״שדי לא מצאנוהו שגיא כח״. (Ibid. 27:20): ״ואתה תצוה את בני ישראל ויקחו אליך שמן זית זך״, and it is written (ibid. 23:19): ״ראשית בכורי אדמתך תביא״ etc. — and when He gives them, He gives according to His power, as it says (Ezekiel 47:12): ״ועל הנחל יעלה על שפתו מזה ומזה כל עץ מאכל לא יבול עלהו ולא יתום פריו לחדשיו יבכר״ — that every single tree produces new first-fruits in every single month, not like the first-fruits of this month [being the same as] the first-fruits of this month. When He asks, He asks only according to their power, as it says (Leviticus 23:40): ״ולקחתם לכם ביום הראשון פרי עץ הדר כפת תמרים״, and when He gives, He gives according to His power, as it says (Isaiah 41:19): ״אתן במדבר ארז שטה והדס ועץ שמן אשים בערבה ברוש תדהר ותאשור יחדו״, and it is written (ibid. 55): ״תחת הנעצוץ יעלה ברוש ותחת הסרפד יעלה הדס״ etc." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'את קרבני לחמי לאשי' — each of these four words alludes to the attribute of judgment (midat ha-din), for it is the speaking [attribute]: he began with 'את' in the sense of (Ezekiel 2:2): ״ואשמע את מדבר אלי״. And likewise 'קרבני' from the root of drawing near (קרב) and war (מלחמה); and likewise 'לאשי' from the sense of ״אשה ריח ניחוח״ — its explanation being that this offering is the drawing down of the descent of influence toward Him. 'תשמרו' — that is, be careful that your intention be toward the unique Name, in order to offer it to Me. 'ואמרת להם' — this offering which you shall offer, I myself, to YHWH, when you offer lambs of the first year, two daily, a perpetual burnt-offering. And this daily offering (תמיד) which the verse ordained is to be offered every single day: one lamb in the morning, and fine flour for a meal-offering, a tenth of an ephah, which is the measure of the omer, and a quarter-hin of wine for the drink-offering; and such was the offering of between the evenings. And it is known that offering these things constitutes the principal foods of man; and since the growth, increase, and maturation of foods comes about through the agency of the two luminaries, which are the sun and the moon, which are the cause of morning and evening, we offer this offering to the Crown of Glory (עטרת תפארת), from which the sun and the moon were emanated, and the dominion which they hold in this lower world — whether in the earthly generations and in all that comes into being from it, or among the children of men — comes to them from there. And because of this, the one was in the morning and the second between the evenings, and not both together. And you already know that the morning daily offering was slaughtered on the west, facing east, and that of between the evenings was slaughtered on the east, facing west, as was explained in the Gemara of Yoma, chapter 'The Goat-Sacrifices of Yom Kippur'; and this is what is meant by 'two daily,' and they expounded there: 'facing the day,' that is, facing the shining of the sun — whether it be the morning one or the one of between the evenings — for the intention is toward the middle line, which is the power of the sun. And this daily offering would atone for and shield Israel, and your sign is (Proverbs 4:9): ״עטרת תפארת תמגנך״. And from this the Masters of Truth, of blessed memory, alluded to us in the blessing of the moon, that they are a crown of glory (עטרת תפארת) to those borne in the womb (לעמוסי בטן). And through this the world was sustained and maintained and the foods were blessed, for the blessing would spread and be drawn from the innermost sanctum (לפני ולפנים) to Eden, and from Eden to the Garden, and from there it would distribute to what is distributed — and understand this."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {3 "Rabbeinu Bahya's prooftext chain alternates between what HaShem asks of Israel and what He gives; the verse in Ezekiel 47:12 about monthly first-fruits is followed by a cryptic phrase 'לא כמגדי חדש זה מגדי חדש זה' which appears to mean that each month's yield is distinct; rendered hyper-literally as the text is elliptical and possibly truncated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:05.385530849Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}